00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:39.023
I'm open now, I'm more interested in,

00:21:39.901 --> 00:21:44.150
I don't know what's involved in this race.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:45.580
And then the next time I got to the gym,

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.930
I was like, maybe you
should tell me a little bit

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:48.780
about this thing that I'm gonna be doing,

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:49.613
this Spartan Race.

00:21:49.613 --> 00:21:51.770
He was like, well we don't
train here, it's like,

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:53.990
you gotta come Sunday
mornings first thing.

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:55.080
We're gonna meet out at a park

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:56.910
and we're gonna do
special training for it.

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:58.490
I'm like okay.

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:02.805
You gotta run a 5K and
there's 20 obstacles

00:22:02.805 --> 00:22:06.000
all along the way and

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:10.050
there's a Spartan Race,
there's a Tough Mudder,

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.389
there's all types of
things floating around

00:22:12.389 --> 00:22:14.730
that I've heard about.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.190
But I'm like okay I'm
gonna go to the training.

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:20.720
I'll see how this shapes up.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:22.080
I didn't really look on the website.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.384
I made up my mind, I didn't
wanna look at the obstacles

00:22:24.384 --> 00:22:27.420
or really try to
understand what's happening

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.713
'cause I felt like I would get nervous.

00:22:29.713 --> 00:22:34.393
So I'm just gonna go to the
training on that Sunday morning.

00:22:34.393 --> 00:22:36.720
- But the obstacles that were gonna be,

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.096
they weren't gonna replicate
the obstacles in this park.

00:22:39.096 --> 00:22:41.690
Not to an exact degree, right?

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:44.160
- No when we went to the park,
there was just a small group

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.510
of us, like six of us,

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:49.327
and the trainer guy, my
friend who owns the gym.

00:22:49.327 --> 00:22:52.200
- Do the obstacles change each year?

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.510
- They change from
location to location, yeah.

00:22:55.510 --> 00:22:57.180
- So you don't really know
what you're getting into,

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:58.013
that's part of it.

00:22:58.013 --> 00:23:00.720
- Yeah but I didn't even
wanna know like generally.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.670
I knew there was mud
involved and they talked

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.980
about crawling under barbed wire.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:06.467
Like well okay.

00:23:06.467 --> 00:23:08.430
And I get there and it's just a park.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.694
There's no obstacles at
all, I mean, it's just,

00:23:10.694 --> 00:23:12.450
there's a dog park over here

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:14.414
and then there's a regular park over here

00:23:14.414 --> 00:23:19.409
and so we have to pair up
and just start running a lap

00:23:19.409 --> 00:23:24.090
around this park, off,
there's no, just on the dirt.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:26.075
And then whenever we
would come back around,

00:23:26.075 --> 00:23:28.940
we'd get down and we'd
start crawling on the dirt

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:31.700
and he had some bags
and we'd carry some bags

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.420
or we'd do burpees.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:35.620
But he was talking about,
now, you gotta get low

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:37.590
and this is how you do an army crawl.

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:39.910
Just imagine there's a barb wire over top,

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:42.610
I'm like dang, am I gonna
be bleeding at this thing?

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:44.650
- Well if you don't get low enough.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.590
- I think at Tough
Mudder, Jenna was telling,

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:50.660
they'll like shock you and stuff.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:51.940
There was none of that for the Spartan.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:52.797
The Spartan Race is not--

00:23:52.797 --> 00:23:54.373
- The Tough Mudder gets up to--

00:23:54.373 --> 00:23:55.995
- It's not as crazy as that.

00:23:55.995 --> 00:23:58.510
- They all have different
levels but the most,

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:02.570
the highest Tough Mudder,
it's like a Tough Mudder.

00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:03.920
- Yeah.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:07.800
So this, you don't get
shocked, but you do get dirty

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:09.590
and you get down in it.

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.445
So like I'm learning
how to do an army crawl

00:24:11.445 --> 00:24:16.110
in the middle of this
park and that's not easy,

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:17.900
you gotta have good hip mobility

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:20.733
to stay low.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.680
And I'm crawling around on my belly

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.590
and all of a sudden I hear somebody say,

00:24:28.447 --> 00:24:29.637
"What are you guys up
to?" and then I said,

00:24:29.637 --> 00:24:31.090
"Oh we're training for a Spartan Race,"

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:32.590
and then I'm crawling up to this guy

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:35.050
and he's like walking
his dog, heckling us,

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:38.967
and he's like, "You pay to
crawl around on your belly

00:24:38.967 --> 00:24:40.440
"out here like that?"

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:44.839
And I'm like, I wanted to say
something very nasty to him.

00:24:44.839 --> 00:24:46.810
I bit my tongue, I didn't say anything.

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:48.330
- He was just trying to make conversation.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:51.090
- And then I'm like, his dog is crapping.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:53.525
- You pay for your dog to crap like that?

00:24:53.525 --> 00:24:55.300
That would have been a bad comeback.

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:57.840
- So here I am crawling, I'm crawling,

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.980
there's like dog crap obstacles.

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:01.947
- Oh, thank you, sir.

00:25:01.947 --> 00:25:04.330
- (chuckles) It's like kinda humiliating.

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:06.732
I was kinda second-guessing my decision

00:25:06.732 --> 00:25:09.540
and then we start running and I realize

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:13.190
I am horrible at running.

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:16.797
Like, I know how to move
my legs like a runner

00:25:16.797 --> 00:25:19.690
but it's something about
the breathing part of it

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:22.000
that is extremely difficult for me.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:24.750
I was out of breath,
man, I was struggling.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:28.210
- I learned this when we ran the mile--

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:29.430
- Yes.
- In our old studio

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:33.640
in a very small circle, and I
thought that you were joking.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.570
I thought that the trouble
that you were having

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:37.100
with it was a bit.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:39.860
- I think I have asthma.
(Rhett snickers)

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:41.530
I mean I don't wanna make light of asthma,

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:43.100
I just can't breathe when I start running.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:46.570
- I think it's a rhythm thing.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:49.800
- Yeah, I mean I was
asking about it, like,

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:50.633
my trainer was like--

00:25:50.633 --> 00:25:52.070
- You're not breathing deeply enough.

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:53.350
- Yeah you gotta, you know,

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:54.709
the breathe in through your nose,

00:25:54.709 --> 00:25:55.608
breathe out through your mouth.

00:25:55.608 --> 00:25:57.863
I remember back when we were

00:25:57.863 --> 00:25:59.836
in little league baseball,

00:25:59.836 --> 00:26:01.675
we'd have to run laps around there.

00:26:01.675 --> 00:26:03.660
It was like breathe in through your nose,

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:05.227
breathe out through your
mouth and I'm just like--

00:26:05.227 --> 00:26:07.800
(panting dramatically)

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:12.800
So we paired up because,
and there's a woman

00:26:13.389 --> 00:26:17.240
who had a French accent
whose name was Blandine.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:18.740
- Wha wha what?
- I've seen her around?

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.080
- She sounds like a graphic
novel star or something,

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:22.880
like Blandine.

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:26.290
- Blandine the French woman
became my workout partner

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:27.410
so I'm running at--

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:29.949
- That's the best name.

00:26:29.949 --> 00:26:32.130
- Great person too.
- That I've ever heard.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:33.740
- Great person.

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:36.450
- I might have a daughter
just so I can have a Blandine.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:39.040
I'm gonna get my vasectomy reversed.

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.111
I'm gonna go to one of those specialists

00:26:41.111 --> 00:26:44.183
that can put your sperm in
a certrifuge to make sure

00:26:44.183 --> 00:26:47.276
that only the female sperm
or whatever you call it,

00:26:47.276 --> 00:26:48.660
get in there.

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.706
I'm gonna have a girl so
I can name her Blandine.

00:26:51.706 --> 00:26:52.981
You heard it right here.
- And you need to set

00:26:52.981 --> 00:26:57.100
the spin cycle to French so
she comes out speaking French.

00:26:57.100 --> 00:26:58.602
- Oh gosh.

00:26:58.602 --> 00:27:00.776
That could probably be
done, it's 20, is it 19?

00:27:00.776 --> 00:27:01.609
It's 2019.
- You know what?

00:27:01.609 --> 00:27:03.609
I never ask her to speak to me in French.

00:27:03.609 --> 00:27:05.448
- (chuckles) Well good.

00:27:05.448 --> 00:27:07.020
- But she has an accent.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:08.010
She's a very nice person.

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:11.490
Her and her husband own
these gyms for kids,

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:13.480
you know how when you take
your kid to this place

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:17.820
and there might be trampolines
and obstacles and crap.

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:19.480
So she was--
- Oh like a jump center?

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:22.493
- Yeah so she's athletic-minded,
and we start running

00:27:22.493 --> 00:27:25.690
and I'm like oh she's setting the pace.

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:27.167
(chuckles)

00:27:27.167 --> 00:27:29.380
- Blandine, gotta keep with Blandine.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:32.310
- Very early I'm like, uh,
I'm not good at running.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.190
I don't think I know how to breathe right.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:38.664
And then a few, like three minutes later,

00:27:38.664 --> 00:27:41.850
I couldn't talk anymore so it
was like I couldn't object,

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:45.000
I just had to really try
to run to keep up with her.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:46.700
- She was your partner though
so it's just you and her.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:48.840
- It's not that she was running fast,

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.696
it's just that she was
running at a decent pace

00:27:50.696 --> 00:27:53.010
and I don't know how to do that.

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:56.200
- Right.
- From a breathing point.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.300
- It could be in your legs too.

00:27:57.300 --> 00:27:58.717
Maybe your legs aren't
moving the right way.

00:27:58.717 --> 00:28:01.751
They have an inefficient
stroke they call it.

00:28:01.751 --> 00:28:05.970
- I achieved fatigue pretty quickly

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.180
but I pushed through just like it says

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:09.890
on the back of my medal.

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:11.330
Push harder.
- Okay.

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:12.820
Almost like it says--
- I'm training, man.

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:13.780
- On the back of your medal.

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:15.347
(chuckles)

00:28:15.347 --> 00:28:20.347
- And when you get really
fatigued, it's hard to run

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:22.903
and I found myself falling.

00:28:23.973 --> 00:28:25.680
We're running on the dirt and--

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.020
- Falling behind or falling?
- Like falling on my face.

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:31.133
Like falling, she was
running a little bit ahead,

00:28:31.133 --> 00:28:33.820
it was a little bit of a
decline, it was like dirt

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:36.315
and gravelish type scenario

00:28:36.315 --> 00:28:39.140
and whenever she would hit a little dip

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:42.620
and then it would go down and
up, she'd get some momentum

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:43.940
so it'd be like woo-woop

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:45.950
and so she could get up the other side.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:48.000
I wasn't great at that either and I fell,

00:28:49.165 --> 00:28:51.431
if it weren't for my hands catching me,

00:28:51.431 --> 00:28:52.920
I would have fallen flat on my face

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:56.100
and like my knees bleeding and
she's like, "Are you okay?"

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:58.599
I'm like, "I'm not good at running."

00:28:58.599 --> 00:28:59.730
(Rhett chuckles)

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:01.980
And I get up, I'm running
the rest, and then,

00:29:02.851 --> 00:29:05.760
so I had a war wound, so
I come back the next week

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.980
and I got a knee brace.
- A knee brace.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:11.200
- Over my knee wound.
- Okay.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.560
- 'Cause my knee was
also hurting after that.

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:15.720
On the inside and the outside.

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.320
And then I'm running the next
time and Blandine's not there

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:20.576
'cause she had to go to
France for the holidays.

00:29:20.576 --> 00:29:22.280
- Yeah of course.
- So I'm running with Lewis.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:23.400
- Quarterly trip to France.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:25.220
- Lewis is even faster than Blandine,

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:26.565
I'm trying to keep up with them.

00:29:26.565 --> 00:29:27.890
(breathing erratically)

00:29:27.890 --> 00:29:29.153
Just like sucking air.

00:29:30.034 --> 00:29:31.810
We're running up in the
woods, we're coming down

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:36.810
from the woods on a decline
and he's well ahead of me

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:40.922
so he's stopping to do our burpees

00:29:40.922 --> 00:29:44.080
and as he turns around and
looks, I give him a show.

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:48.640
Like I splayed out and
just (exhales sharply)

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:50.170
fell again.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:52.340
On my hands and my knees,

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:55.470
I'm just like tumbling down this hill.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:58.930
All because I can't run, I'm that tired.

00:29:58.930 --> 00:30:01.038
I get so tired that my
feet wouldn't lift off

00:30:01.038 --> 00:30:03.100
of the dirt.

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:05.230
And he's like, uh.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:08.043
You don't know what to say
in that instance to the guy.

00:30:08.043 --> 00:30:09.580
It's like you're training with this guy

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:12.010
and he's like, "Uh, you okay, man?"

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:15.198
And I'm like, "Yeah, I
did this last week too."

00:30:15.198 --> 00:30:17.240
- (chuckles) I did this with Blandine.

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:18.690
- So then the next week I show up,

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.230
I got a knee brace on both knees.

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:22.807
- You need like a full body suit.

00:30:22.807 --> 00:30:26.713
You need like a Kevlar three-piece suit.

00:30:26.713 --> 00:30:29.869
- I need to look like somebody
that you do motion capture--

00:30:29.869 --> 00:30:32.650
- Well what you need is
you need a four-wheeler

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:35.240
just to ride and follow everyone. (laughs)

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:36.800
- I need to begin efficient.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.500
- I got a helmet and I
got a combustible engine

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:40.880
that propels me.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:45.010
- So I became, I developed this
reputation amongst the group

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.950
of being the guy who is
gonna bleed every time.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:50.680
- The falling bleeder.
(Link chuckles)

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:54.895
- Needless to say, come race
day, I was pretty nervous.

00:30:54.895 --> 00:30:55.793
- Right.

00:30:57.287 --> 00:30:59.616
- That didn't affect my
sleep because you know,

00:30:59.616 --> 00:31:02.813
whenever my head hits
the pillow, I get stupid.

00:31:02.813 --> 00:31:04.611
I am a stupid sleeper.

00:31:04.611 --> 00:31:06.980
So I'm blessed with that but
when I woke up I was like,

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:08.937
man I'm kinda nervous.

00:31:08.937 --> 00:31:11.990
We had to drive out to Chino
or Chico or what, Chino.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:14.100
- Yeah you gotta know
which one you're going to.

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:15.560
- A lot of people out there.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:17.670
I'm like oh I get to see these obstacles

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:18.807
and everything and--

00:31:19.936 --> 00:31:22.370
- There's probably a lot of
falling bleeders out there too.

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:26.510
- I had on both of my knee braces.

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:28.618
They gave us socks so we
could all look the same

00:31:28.618 --> 00:31:32.950
so I got these tube socks on my shins

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:35.220
and got a headband.

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:36.680
I always wear a headband, by the way.

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:37.810
That's my signature.
- I've heard about this.

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:39.160
- Every time I work out, I gotta--

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:40.320
- There's always one in every gym.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:42.570
- I'm that guy. (chuckles)

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:43.970
- The guy with the headband.

00:31:45.404 --> 00:31:47.460
- So--
- I usually don't talk to him,

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:49.253
but I would talk to you.

00:31:50.150 --> 00:31:52.920
- Blandine and I take off, the race starts

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:57.160
and I'm like okay, I can do this.

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:58.660
I caught a glimpse of
some of the obstacles.

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:01.390
There's a three-foot wall you gotta

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:02.490
hoist yourself over that.

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.836
But there's like a five-foot wall

00:32:04.836 --> 00:32:06.710
that like I'm hoisting myself up, well,

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:11.640
I put my foot out, Blandine
jumps off of my thigh

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:13.420
and I boost her over.

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:14.630
- Right 'cause--
- There's a teamwork--

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:16.680
- Mentality, and the
rest of our team there,

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.456
they decided to go at a
slower pace, and you know--

00:32:19.456 --> 00:32:20.400
- Whoa whoa, hold on.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:21.997
You went Blandine pace?
- I went Blandine pace.

00:32:21.997 --> 00:32:23.240
- The day of the race?

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:24.715
- You know, I been training some more.

00:32:24.715 --> 00:32:26.663
- Hold on, but was this
discussed ahead of time?

00:32:26.663 --> 00:32:31.510
- Yeah, yeah I was like
all right, don't leave me.

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:32.940
Please.
- Just you and her?

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:34.260
- Yeah.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:36.900
And there's--
- So there's no obstacles

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:39.098
where you need, I've--
- No.

00:32:39.098 --> 00:32:40.480
- I've read a little bit about it

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:43.760
and I've seen pictures
where you gotta have

00:32:43.760 --> 00:32:45.620
like three people minimum

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:47.890
to get over these certain
things or something.

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:51.240
- Only if this is like your first race

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:53.310
and you need help over
things does your team

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:56.351
rally around you to help
you get over things.

00:32:56.351 --> 00:32:58.520
But for us we just had each other

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:01.632
and a lot of people were
just kinda paired up.

00:33:01.632 --> 00:33:06.632
And then there's like,
you're climbing a net wall

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.790
and when I get up to
the top, I'm like man,

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:12.700
this is, it hits me that like,
if I were to fall from this,

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:13.603
I could die.

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:18.890
I gotta really be careful
and I got more scared.

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:20.320
And then we're going
over all types of stuff

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:23.840
and then there's a mud hill.

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:27.086
I'd say it's probably a six-feet tall,

00:33:27.086 --> 00:33:31.870
muddy hill and you climb this
thing and you get on the top

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:34.400
and you slide down into muddy water.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:36.170
- Mm-hmm.
- It's like three feet deep

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:38.706
and you're trudging across that.

00:33:38.706 --> 00:33:42.050
Another muddy hill, it's taller.

00:33:42.050 --> 00:33:42.883
- Was it cold?

00:33:44.574 --> 00:33:46.320
- Conditions were perfect.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.200
I would say it was like
74 degrees in the air.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:53.870
- 'Cause that would be, cold
would be the worst part.

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.540
- So I get up on the second
hill and we're sliding down,

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:58.300
I'm like man, this is fun.

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:00.425
All of a sudden I'm having fun.

00:34:00.425 --> 00:34:02.210
I'm not as scared anymore.

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:06.270
And so I slide down in that mud pit.

00:34:06.270 --> 00:34:08.142
- Are you like saying woo?

00:34:08.142 --> 00:34:10.610
Did you have anything that
you say when you get over a--

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:13.367
- I didn't have like fun
exclamations like yeehaw

00:34:13.367 --> 00:34:14.680
or anything like that.

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:18.738
I kept my redneck inner dialogue inside.

00:34:18.738 --> 00:34:20.890
- What kinds of things did you say?

00:34:20.890 --> 00:34:22.771
- I went down there and I'm like,

00:34:22.771 --> 00:34:25.920
my face didn't get muddy
but my shoulders did

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:29.797
and I'm in up to my
armpits and I turn around

00:34:29.797 --> 00:34:33.198
and this big football player dude

00:34:33.198 --> 00:34:35.780
makes a huge splash and when I turn around

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:37.760
and look at him, he has gone under.

00:34:37.760 --> 00:34:40.400
He's got mud all over his face.

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:43.947
And I look at him and I
say, "You look great."

00:34:44.943 --> 00:34:47.040
And--
- You encouraged someone.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:48.270
- I encouraged someone.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:50.620
- But you also seemed
like kinda making fun

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:51.800
of him a little bit.

00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:56.010
- And he looked at me and I
noticed that he was struggling.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:58.560
He's like wiping his
eyes with his muddy hands

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:00.770
and he was like, I got it in my eyes.

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:03.012
So he thought I was taunting him.

00:35:03.012 --> 00:35:05.330
'Cause he was like having a moment.

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:06.770
- Yeah well that--
- I was like you look great.

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:08.700
- He was like, I got
it all in my eyes, man,

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:10.790
and he was upset.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:11.956
And I was like oh--

00:35:11.956 --> 00:35:14.025
- You sounded like you were
kinda making fun of him,

00:35:14.025 --> 00:35:16.110
just so you know.
- I wasn't trying to.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:17.510
- Right.
- But I did realize that

00:35:17.510 --> 00:35:18.660
he was in duress in that moment.

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:20.690
I tried to make up for it and I was like,

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:24.167
my sleeve doesn't have any mud on it.

00:35:24.167 --> 00:35:27.070
Wipe your eyes on my sleeve, man.

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:29.160
And he was like, nah,
nah, nah, I was like dude.

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:30.300
- Was Blandine okay with this?

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:31.670
Where was she?
- She was trying

00:35:31.670 --> 00:35:33.410
to go up the next hill.

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.973
- She's like, quit
helping the competition!

00:35:35.973 --> 00:35:37.600
- And so he was like nah, man.

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:40.500
I'm like, no, it's perfectly dry.

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:43.540
Just wipe your eyes on my sleeve, man.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:46.960
And I basically made
him do it and he did it

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.840
and then he didn't say anything to me.

00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:52.390
He just went up the hill.

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:54.210
And then so I go up the next hill--

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:55.510
- You Linked him.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:57.870
You totally Linked him.
- I helped him!

00:35:57.870 --> 00:35:59.850
- You unintentionally--
- I helped a blind man

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:00.740
on the road.

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:02.410
- You unintentionally insulted him.

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:04.700
That's Link step number one.
- He looked great.

00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:06.660
- And then you forced him--
- He couldn't see.

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:08.340
- You forced him into doing something

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:10.817
that he didn't wanna do
which is Link number two.

00:36:10.817 --> 00:36:13.635
(chuckling)

00:36:13.635 --> 00:36:16.070
And then you told the
story and didn't realize

00:36:16.070 --> 00:36:16.903
that (laughs)--

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:20.600
- Well it ain't--
- How he took it.

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:22.060
That's Link number three.

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:23.900
- And then I get up to the
next hill and I realize,

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:27.557
oh I gotta slide down this
third one into more mud

00:36:27.557 --> 00:36:32.290
and now there is an
inflatable wall that is--

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.580
- Well that sounds fun.
- That is brought down

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:36.050
to the surface of the mud

00:36:36.980 --> 00:36:39.552
and they force you to
submerge yourself completely

00:36:39.552 --> 00:36:42.039
and go under, so everything
I did to help that guy

00:36:42.039 --> 00:36:43.670
was immediately erased by the fact

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:46.140
that we all just have
to go under this thing.

00:36:46.140 --> 00:36:47.270
- That's what he should have said.

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:49.810
- And he knew that because
he told me once we got

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:51.380
to the top of the hill, he was like,

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:53.810
this is a lot easier
than it was yesterday.

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:55.060
I was like, you did this yesterday?

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:56.580
He was like yeah.

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:59.570
Like oh so you knew that you
were about to go under all,

00:36:59.570 --> 00:37:03.090
go under the mud and
basically me forcing you

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:05.340
to wipe your face on
my shirt was pointless.

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:06.890
- Yeah.
- I didn't say any of that,

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:07.780
I just kinda--
- Thought it.

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:09.093
- Slinked along.
- Yeah.

00:37:09.093 --> 00:37:11.664
- But we had to submerge
ourselves completely in the mud

00:37:11.664 --> 00:37:15.320
and that was a fun moment.

00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:16.370
But then right after that,

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:18.910
we're running to the next obstacle

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:23.910
and it is a, like a 45
degree wooden triangle

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:27.630
that was probably eight feet tall

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:29.690
and it had ropes hanging from it

00:37:29.690 --> 00:37:32.666
and it was soaked because
everybody was soaked

00:37:32.666 --> 00:37:35.180
and your feet are soaked,
your whole body's soaked

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:37.535
by this point and you
have to grab the rope,

00:37:37.535 --> 00:37:42.535
stand on the wood and walk up
this wet inversion obstacle.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:48.900
And that was--
- Well how did you do it--

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:50.910
- Nearly impossible.
- If you can't have no grip?

00:37:50.910 --> 00:37:53.340
- Well you could grip the
rope but then your feet had

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:57.784
to grip but you had to lean
back in order to create more,

00:37:57.784 --> 00:37:59.637
what's that called, in statics.

00:37:59.637 --> 00:38:01.450
- Friction?

00:38:01.450 --> 00:38:06.450
- Well you know, you gotta,
that force back towards,

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:08.340
if you lean back on the rope,

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:10.690
your legs become like a moment arm

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:15.690
and you're sending your force
vector back into the wood.

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:20.640
So that you can get
more traction and walk.

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:23.210
But it was, people were getting halfway up

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:24.700
and then falling flat on their faces

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.540
and then sliding back down

00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:28.238
into all the people that were waiting.

00:38:28.238 --> 00:38:29.554
And I was determined--
- Now if you get up

00:38:29.554 --> 00:38:32.750
to a obstacle like this,
is there somebody there

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:33.830
to make sure you go over?

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:36.994
- No, well there's people
like on the sides saying,

00:38:36.994 --> 00:38:38.600
hey just go around.

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:41.236
If you can't do an obstacle,
you have to do 30 burpees.

00:38:41.236 --> 00:38:43.050
- Oh.
- But you lose a lot

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:44.500
of pride when you gotta do the burpees.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:46.270
30 burpees is actually not easy.

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:47.103
- Not easy.

00:38:48.309 --> 00:38:50.810
- I was determined to
make it up and I noticed

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:53.120
that there was one rope
that had three knots in it

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:54.930
all the way up, some
didn't have any knots.

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:56.290
I'm like I'm going for that one.

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:59.863
I get halfway up and then the
space between the second knot

00:38:59.863 --> 00:39:03.900
and the third knot was
farther than I could reach

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:07.090
with my fingertips so
I would grab the rope

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:08.980
and just slide back down

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:11.050
and you feel like everybody's watching,

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:12.660
everybody's waiting for you to get over.

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:14.244
- Right 'cause one rope.
- Well there's,

00:39:14.244 --> 00:39:16.540
probably five people could go at a time,

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:19.072
but there's probably 45 people waiting--

00:39:19.072 --> 00:39:19.905
- Oh man.

00:39:19.905 --> 00:39:20.738
- 'Cause people start backing up.

00:39:20.738 --> 00:39:22.240
- Embarrassing.

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:24.030
- I was desperate to get over this thing

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:26.510
and I was holding on the
rope, I was leaning back,

00:39:26.510 --> 00:39:29.520
I was like really struggling and I'm like,

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.150
I look at a rope over here
and it has a knot on it

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:34.970
and I'm like, belaying
over and putting my foot

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:38.490
on the knot in the other
rope and trying desperately

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:40.650
to push up and there's
a guy right up there

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:44.360
who's helping some people over
but he's not helping me over.

00:39:44.360 --> 00:39:47.950
He's just like, and I don't
know if he was waiting for me

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:49.020
to reach out--
- It was 'cause

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:50.390
of the headband.

00:39:50.390 --> 00:39:51.630
- Well we all wore headbands.

00:39:51.630 --> 00:39:53.290
- Oh.
- They give you a headband

00:39:53.290 --> 00:39:54.380
with a number on it.

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:56.540
- Oh it was your number then.
- Yeah that's why I trained

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:57.460
with a headband.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:39:58.943
- Do you have athletic glasses?

00:39:58.943 --> 00:40:00.950
Like Kurt Rambis?

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:02.380
- I was wearing contacts.

00:40:02.380 --> 00:40:04.653
I wasn't wearing Kurt Rambis goggles.

00:40:05.522 --> 00:40:07.750
- Missed opportunity.
- I did make it over

00:40:07.750 --> 00:40:11.210
but it was harrowing.

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:13.337
And then I hung at the top
and like grabbed Blandine

00:40:13.337 --> 00:40:15.650
and pulled her over, it was
like a survival situation,

00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:19.780
I felt like, it's like an adult version

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:23.793
of don't step in the lava
kind of a vibe was happening.

00:40:25.505 --> 00:40:27.913
There's a lot more obstacles after that

00:40:27.913 --> 00:40:31.170
but that was the most
difficult spot for me.

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.160
After that, there's some
rings and other monkey bars,

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:37.530
I'm like carrying a lot of weight

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:41.246
in a bucket around a circuit
and all these different things.

00:40:41.246 --> 00:40:44.780
There was even a javelin toss.

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:48.650
Like a Spartan, like a, is that
what it's called, a javelin?

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:50.170
Like a stick that--
- Yeah, javelin.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:52.420
- You could skewer somebody.
- Into what?

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:54.660
- Into a--
- Hay bale?

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.580
- Into people who were doing burpees.

00:40:57.580 --> 00:40:59.020
- Oh good.
- Yeah it's like

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:01.960
oh you can't throw the javelin,
then you're the target.

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:04.060
- Kill the burpee-ers.
- Like a hay bale.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.680
Turns out we ran the whole
time from obstacle to obstacle.

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:12.660
We didn't do any walking,
some people were doing that.

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:15.100
If you went first thing in the morning,

00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:17.270
you were in the elite competition people

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:18.650
who were like doing this time

00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:21.508
and that's like American
Ninja Warrior type vibe,

00:41:21.508 --> 00:41:25.430
but this was like the
free-for-all, some people walking,

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:26.990
everybody going at their own pace.

00:41:26.990 --> 00:41:28.927
So it wasn't the competitive run

00:41:28.927 --> 00:41:31.143
to get across the finish line first.

00:41:33.309 --> 00:41:35.670
But once it gets to the end
there's like a rope you have

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:39.320
to climb and that's the
first thing I could not do.

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:42.430
I'd never learned the
technique to climb a rope.

00:41:42.430 --> 00:41:44.000
Blandine was trying to show me

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:45.260
and she was having a difficult time

00:41:45.260 --> 00:41:46.980
so we ended up doing the burpees.

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.630
And then I saw--
- How high do you have to go?

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:52.935
- Probably 15 feet.

00:41:52.935 --> 00:41:53.935
- That'd be trouble.

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:57.580
- It was intimidating.

00:41:57.580 --> 00:42:00.330
And it really, my hands were hurting.

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:01.800
So I was like I'll just do the burpees

00:42:01.800 --> 00:42:03.250
and then I saw Christy and the kids

00:42:03.250 --> 00:42:05.840
and they saw me cross the finish line.

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:09.166
And we got our pictures
taken, they gave me the medal

00:42:09.166 --> 00:42:13.250
and there's people there at
the end who hand you the medal,

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:15.330
put it around your neck.

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:19.551
And so I knew that my family was watching.

00:42:19.551 --> 00:42:22.580
I felt like the whole world was watching.

00:42:22.580 --> 00:42:26.900
So I decided I'm gonna run up to this guy

00:42:26.900 --> 00:42:28.520
who's handing me the medal--
- Uh-oh.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:31.273
- And I'm going to take
a knee like a knight.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:35.660
So I went to take a knee.

00:42:35.660 --> 00:42:37.150
Nobody else was taking a knee.

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:40.120
I guess the guy hadn't (chuckles)

00:42:40.120 --> 00:42:41.780
encountered anyone taking a knee

00:42:41.780 --> 00:42:46.030
because as I took the knee,
he tried to put the medal

00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:49.749
over where my head used to be and instead,

00:42:49.749 --> 00:42:54.270
slapped me square in
the face with my medal.

00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:55.330
- That's kinda what you deserve.

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:58.379
- Which is a heavy medal.

00:42:58.379 --> 00:43:01.230
This is an impressively
heavy medal and I know

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:05.660
because it hit me square in
the face at the end of my race.

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:06.800
- Wow.
- I'm pretty sure Christy

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:08.470
got this on video.
- I hope she did.

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:09.950
- I haven't watched it back but if it is,

00:43:09.950 --> 00:43:12.150
we can cut to it for the video version.

00:43:12.150 --> 00:43:15.633
YouTube.com/EarBiscuits, subscribe now.

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:20.020
So that kinda put a damper on my ending,

00:43:20.020 --> 00:43:22.041
it's just like wham right in the face.

00:43:22.041 --> 00:43:24.580
(Rhett chuckling)
And then we ran,

00:43:24.580 --> 00:43:26.000
we got some pictures but then--

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:27.530
- Oh gosh.
- We actually ran,

00:43:27.530 --> 00:43:28.744
I was exhausted.

00:43:28.744 --> 00:43:31.880
I was absolutely exhausted
but Blandine was like

00:43:31.880 --> 00:43:36.880
let's run back and come through
with the rest of our team.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:39.460
We can support them, we're
all in this together.

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:42.940
I'm like, that's a great
idea but I hate you

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:44.830
for thinking that is what I was thinking.

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:47.230
I don't want to run back, and
then we started running back,

00:43:47.230 --> 00:43:51.940
and both of my calves
cramped up, I couldn't move,

00:43:51.940 --> 00:43:53.990
I'm like, I gotta, she's running back.

00:43:53.990 --> 00:43:56.220
I'm like, I can't do this.

00:43:56.220 --> 00:44:00.190
But we did catch up with them
and we brought them back--

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:02.457
- They ended up carrying you. (chuckles)

00:44:02.457 --> 00:44:04.110
Over the--
- I recovered but

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:06.780
we got to the rope climb at
the end and Blandine wants

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:07.910
to try it again, she's like--

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:10.502
- I like Blandine's attitude.
- Got this inner competition.

00:44:10.502 --> 00:44:13.300
And of course my family's
still there watching

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:16.312
and then everybody's like, try
it again, do the rope climb--

00:44:16.312 --> 00:44:17.770
- Oh gosh.
- I'm like I don't wanna

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:18.671
do this again.

00:44:18.671 --> 00:44:22.950
I didn't do it the first
time, and so, I had watched,

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:25.590
and I just, the technique
is you're supposed

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:27.510
to climb a rope with your legs,

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:29.157
not your arms.
- Not your arms, yeah.

00:44:29.157 --> 00:44:32.900
- And I started doing it,
like I hooked my feet together

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:36.597
and then I pushed up and
then I reached my hands up

00:44:36.597 --> 00:44:38.410
and I grabbed again.

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:41.151
Actually yeah, I pushed
up with my legs and then,

00:44:41.151 --> 00:44:45.496
I did it a second time and then
all of a sudden, I realize,

00:44:45.496 --> 00:44:49.454
people were cheering and
I did it a third time

00:44:49.454 --> 00:44:53.610
and then I did it a fourth
time and then I looked up

00:44:53.610 --> 00:44:54.600
and there's the bell.

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:56.160
- You got it!
- And I'm ringing

00:44:56.160 --> 00:44:59.633
the freakin' bell, I climbed
the freakin' rope, man!

00:45:01.150 --> 00:45:02.590
Right?
- That's awesome.

00:45:02.590 --> 00:45:04.471
- (laughs) I'm forcing
Rhett to say that's awesome.

00:45:04.471 --> 00:45:09.471
But it was unexpectedly
awesome to actually hit

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:13.400
that bell like when I got
down, my family was cheering

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:14.960
for me and then we crossed the finish line

00:45:14.960 --> 00:45:16.360
and we all took the pictures

00:45:18.319 --> 00:45:21.702
and then it was over and then we went to,

00:45:21.702 --> 00:45:23.740
there's this--
- Ruby Tuesday's.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:27.650
- Well we were so dirty,
like coated head to foot

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:32.290
and they had rigged up
all these garden hoses,

00:45:32.290 --> 00:45:35.045
I'm talking like probably 35 garden hoses

00:45:35.045 --> 00:45:39.107
under this cement slab
and so I go under there

00:45:39.107 --> 00:45:42.280
and we're all hosing off so then we can go

00:45:42.280 --> 00:45:44.971
into this changing place
and change and I look down

00:45:44.971 --> 00:45:49.971
and somebody had left two
hotel-size shampoo bottles--

00:45:51.297 --> 00:45:53.760
- Hmm.
- In front of me,

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:57.014
and there's like probably 50
people at different stages

00:45:57.014 --> 00:46:01.882
of hosing themselves off,
men, women, children.

00:46:01.882 --> 00:46:05.323
It was like post-apocalyptic except for me

00:46:05.323 --> 00:46:09.410
because I was lathered from head to toe

00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:13.120
in every frickin' bit of that shampoo

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:18.120
that nobody else had so I
realized everybody was looking

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:21.340
at me and laughing 'cause I was like,

00:46:21.340 --> 00:46:24.090
I was sudsed up like a
dude taking a bubble bath.

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:24.960
- They didn't wanna use it?

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:27.402
- I offered it to the
people that I knew and like,

00:46:27.402 --> 00:46:30.230
Chad took a little bit and
shampooed his hair a little bit,

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:32.906
I'm like, man, I've never been,

00:46:32.906 --> 00:46:35.950
I can't ever remember being this dirty.

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:37.630
It was like the ram in the thicket.

00:46:37.630 --> 00:46:41.230
God gave me shampoo

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:46.023
and I tried to share it but no
one wanted to go full lather.

00:46:47.162 --> 00:46:48.420
Except me.
- They were like,

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:50.930
I'll do a thorough shower when I get home.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:53.460
- Right, I was like
putting it down my pants

00:46:53.460 --> 00:46:56.650
and then I changed and we--
- Wow.

00:46:56.650 --> 00:46:59.200
- Went back to my family
and ate some beef jerky

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:02.930
and some bananas and headed out of there.

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:07.930
But the thing that happened
that I didn't anticipate was,

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:13.120
and it started at the rope
climb and then it continued

00:47:14.096 --> 00:47:18.083
way into the evening and it was this.

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:23.913
I felt good.
(Rhett chuckles)

00:47:23.913 --> 00:47:27.968
I was proud of what I had done

00:47:27.968 --> 00:47:31.100
and I know that sounds kinda cheesy

00:47:31.100 --> 00:47:34.720
and a little weird to say but
the thing that I realized was

00:47:37.410 --> 00:47:41.430
I in no way anticipated
actually feeling that, honestly.

00:47:41.430 --> 00:47:44.460
I thought I'm gonna sign
up for this thing, yeah,

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:45.620
it's gonna be difficult.

00:47:45.620 --> 00:47:48.560
I'm not surprised that I'm
nervous about doing it.

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:51.110
I'm gonna push myself physically

00:47:51.110 --> 00:47:53.880
and I'm gonna have a physical experience

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:58.380
of completing something, as
long as I don't get hurt,

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:00.323
this is a success.

00:48:01.200 --> 00:48:04.356
But I honestly, it didn't cross my mind

00:48:04.356 --> 00:48:08.490
that there would be an emotional component

00:48:08.490 --> 00:48:10.050
to completing a race like this.

00:48:10.050 --> 00:48:12.020
You know, people talk
about completing marathons

00:48:12.020 --> 00:48:15.341
and I totally get if you run 26 miles

00:48:15.341 --> 00:48:17.849
and you cross a finish line, I totally get

00:48:17.849 --> 00:48:20.860
that you might be in tears of joy.

00:48:20.860 --> 00:48:22.320
- Of course.

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:25.719
- But I did not anticipate
with doing a little three mile,

00:48:25.719 --> 00:48:30.719
5K, jumping over some ropes
and oh, this is no big deal

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:35.840
that it would actually have
an impact on my self-esteem.

00:48:38.483 --> 00:48:40.383
It was surprising to me.

00:48:42.278 --> 00:48:46.510
And it was kinda awesome and I--

00:48:46.510 --> 00:48:49.460
- Does this mean you're
like a Spartan guy now?

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:50.840
- I'm a Spartan junkie now.

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:53.340
No, I've talked to people
after and they're like,

00:48:53.340 --> 00:48:54.260
did you get the bug?

00:48:54.260 --> 00:48:55.620
And I actually know what
they're talking about

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:59.593
'cause there's this thrill
of achieving something.

00:49:00.578 --> 00:49:05.578
I'm not competitive at all so any

00:49:05.626 --> 00:49:07.680
of my many soccer exploits,

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:12.680
I don't know that I, or any type of sport,

00:49:13.367 --> 00:49:15.700
physical competition type thing,

00:49:15.700 --> 00:49:20.700
I don't think I've ever
experienced actual joy in doing it.

00:49:20.764 --> 00:49:21.940
- Hmm.
- Because I was like,

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:23.280
what am I feeling right now?

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:25.130
This is so weird.

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:27.939
What is the feeling that I have

00:49:27.939 --> 00:49:30.870
that my family just
watched me climb a rope?

00:49:30.870 --> 00:49:33.040
I mean it's kinda silly.

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:35.930
I'm a 40 year old guy ringing a cowbell

00:49:35.930 --> 00:49:40.920
at the top of the rope
and the answer was pride.

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:42.637
You know, I was proud.

00:49:42.637 --> 00:49:44.073
I was proud.

00:49:44.073 --> 00:49:48.430
And it was like, it was
so weird to then realize

00:49:48.430 --> 00:49:50.118
that it was the first
time that it happened

00:49:50.118 --> 00:49:52.700
so now I gotta get my other three.

00:49:52.700 --> 00:49:55.110
That night, it was like late that night

00:49:55.110 --> 00:49:58.300
'cause I went into the kitchen

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:01.493
to get a pseudo midnight snack.

00:50:01.493 --> 00:50:03.010
'Cause I had earned it, I mean,

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:03.950
let's be real.
- Yeah, right.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:06.890
- My watch told me I had
burned 1100 calories.

00:50:06.890 --> 00:50:09.410
Took me an hour and a
half to finish the race,

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:10.750
that's how long it took.

00:50:10.750 --> 00:50:12.570
I was absolutely exhausted.

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:14.581
- And is it the same, 'cause
you were telling me that

00:50:14.581 --> 00:50:17.257
your portion wasn't competitive
so people weren't like

00:50:17.257 --> 00:50:19.270
going for time.
- Nobody was shoving.

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:21.080
- But the people before that

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:23.615
in the morning are in the same obstacles?

00:50:23.615 --> 00:50:25.330
- Yes, same obstacles.

00:50:25.330 --> 00:50:27.490
- Give me the--
- I think the record--

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:29.430
- The best time.
- 30 minutes, I think.

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:30.660
Something around there.

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:32.969
- Yeah.
- That's pretty crazy.

00:50:32.969 --> 00:50:36.860
I mean, for me to be running
the whole time, jogging,

00:50:36.860 --> 00:50:38.720
if I'm not doing an
obstacles, and only waiting

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:40.957
at a couple of 'em, it
was an hour and a half

00:50:40.957 --> 00:50:43.000
and I felt really good about that,

00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:45.680
if I haven't established that already.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:48.963
So that night, lo and behold
Lily comes in the kitchen.

00:50:48.963 --> 00:50:51.270
I guess she earned a midnight snack too

00:50:51.270 --> 00:50:53.290
and I'm like, hey Lily.

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:56.320
What did you think about my race today?

00:50:56.320 --> 00:50:58.170
She's like, well you know...

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:03.583
After the rope climb, which
the first time I didn't do it,

00:51:03.583 --> 00:51:05.760
she was like you go up to
the rings and you know how

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:07.435
you gotta stand there
for a second, it's like,

00:51:07.435 --> 00:51:09.113
I kinda looked at your
face and I could tell

00:51:09.113 --> 00:51:11.480
that you were afraid.

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:13.560
(both laugh)

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:14.700
And I'm like, I look at Lily, I'm--

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:16.400
- I can see the fear in your eyes.

00:51:17.410 --> 00:51:19.940
- And I told Lily, I was like, I was.

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:21.577
And she was like, and
then you started doing it

00:51:21.577 --> 00:51:24.100
and you grabbed one
ring and then you swung

00:51:24.100 --> 00:51:26.480
to the next ring and I could
tell you kinda got the hang

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:27.900
of it and then you felt better about it.

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:29.673
And I was like, I did.

00:51:33.030 --> 00:51:34.390
And then after you did,

00:51:34.390 --> 00:51:36.198
and I was like, did
you see me do the rope?

00:51:36.198 --> 00:51:38.350
And she was like, yeah yeah
yeah, when you went back

00:51:38.350 --> 00:51:41.520
and did the rope, that was
cool, and then there was a pause

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:42.370
and she was like,

00:51:43.370 --> 00:51:46.020
I can tell you're pretty
proud of yourself.

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:47.700
(Rhett laughs)
And I'm like--

00:51:47.700 --> 00:51:50.110
- You think?
(both laughing)

00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:51.933
- And I'm like, I am.

00:51:51.933 --> 00:51:53.830
I am, and I was...

00:51:56.711 --> 00:51:59.150
It's the stuff that I've already said

00:51:59.150 --> 00:52:02.720
but it's also this thing of being,

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:04.890
don't you feel like we
were taught a little bit

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:08.270
that like, being proud of yourself

00:52:08.270 --> 00:52:10.267
for doing something is not,

00:52:10.267 --> 00:52:13.990
it's not a pure thing to feel.

00:52:13.990 --> 00:52:16.440
- Not in the McLaughlin
household. (chuckles)

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:17.707
- You think?

00:52:17.707 --> 00:52:22.707
Okay then maybe it's just
me then that I feel like

00:52:23.070 --> 00:52:24.513
if you're gonna be proud
of something you did,

00:52:24.513 --> 00:52:25.950
it's like, yeah I'm proud of that.

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:26.903
There's a tinge of--
- Well no I mean--

00:52:26.903 --> 00:52:28.017
- But you don't wanna flaunt it.

00:52:28.017 --> 00:52:31.200
You don't wanna be honest
and be like yeah tell me--

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.064
- Being proud of yourself
and being prideful--

00:52:35.064 --> 00:52:37.010
- I understand there's
two different things

00:52:37.010 --> 00:52:39.550
but I guess it bled in my psyche.

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:42.720
- Well yeah, yeah yeah.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:46.100
- So I think I will do it again.

00:52:46.100 --> 00:52:47.970
I encourage you to give it a shot.

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:50.952
- Well I was reading that book.

00:52:50.952 --> 00:52:55.090
There's a book about The Iceman Wim Hof--

00:52:55.090 --> 00:52:57.014
- Mm-hmm.
- And the guy who wrote

00:52:57.014 --> 00:52:59.590
the book is talking about...

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:02.100
I read this like a year and a half ago

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:03.470
and he's talking about Spartan Races.

00:53:03.470 --> 00:53:06.110
I need to, I should really
do something like that,

00:53:06.110 --> 00:53:08.550
but they don't do that at my gym,

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:11.620
so I didn't look at it, 'cause you gotta,

00:53:11.620 --> 00:53:12.710
I wasn't gonna

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:15.260
go there by myself.
- Yeah you gotta have a squad.

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:18.910
I don't think you can
be in my squad because--

00:53:18.910 --> 00:53:21.220
- Oh I'm gonna get another
squad and beat your squad.

00:53:21.220 --> 00:53:22.260
- Yeah let's do that.

00:53:22.260 --> 00:53:23.600
- Yeah, I'm gonna take the--

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:25.304
- You're gonna try to recruit Blandine--

00:53:25.304 --> 00:53:27.104
- You and Blandine.
- To your side.

00:53:28.480 --> 00:53:30.750
- No, I'd definitely do it.

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:33.610
I am legitimately, now, you know me,

00:53:33.610 --> 00:53:35.981
I am incredibly competitive.

00:53:35.981 --> 00:53:40.981
Like competitive sports
were a huge part of my life

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:46.410
growing up and so I think
I definitely approach it

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:48.140
very differently because I'd never,

00:53:48.140 --> 00:53:53.120
I didn't think about sports and
competition in the way that,

00:53:53.120 --> 00:53:55.380
I just saw something and it was like,

00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:58.730
I am going to win at
that and I was just like,

00:53:58.730 --> 00:54:02.060
I put everything into how am I gonna win,

00:54:02.060 --> 00:54:04.820
so it was a different kind of approach.

00:54:04.820 --> 00:54:07.683
- But does that mean, can you quantify,

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:11.810
can you pinpoint what's
your greatest physical feat?

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:14.110
'Cause I guess I'm
saying, I mean definitely

00:54:14.110 --> 00:54:17.910
in recent history, this
Spartan Race was that for me,

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:20.550
like a personal physical milestone.

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:24.753
Last time I ran 5K was when
I was on the soccer team,

00:54:24.753 --> 00:54:26.920
like we had to run a lot in high school,

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:28.790
I'm talking that many years ago,

00:54:28.790 --> 00:54:32.940
20 some plus years ago
was the last time I ran

00:54:32.940 --> 00:54:34.880
anywhere near that.

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.016
- Okay so speaking of 5K, so,

00:54:37.016 --> 00:54:39.732
I probably have told
this story maybe way back

00:54:39.732 --> 00:54:41.880
on the RhettandLinKast or something but

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:48.460
my basketball coach was
the cross-country coach.

00:54:48.460 --> 00:54:50.110
Coach Gage.
- Mm-hmm.

00:54:50.110 --> 00:54:52.853
- And still the basketball
coach at Harnett Central.

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:55.380
I don't know if he's
still doing cross-country,

00:54:55.380 --> 00:54:57.630
but anyway, so he kinda got all the guys

00:54:57.630 --> 00:54:59.270
on the basketball team
to run cross-country

00:54:59.270 --> 00:55:04.270
and I was an athlete so I was like okay,

00:55:04.273 --> 00:55:06.140
I'll do this.

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:07.900
I had never really done
long-distance running

00:55:07.900 --> 00:55:11.350
but I was like okay, I
mean people can do it

00:55:11.350 --> 00:55:13.040
so I will be one of the
people who can do it.

00:55:13.040 --> 00:55:15.420
It's the way I've always,
if somebody can do it,

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:16.930
okay I can do it.

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:18.640
- And the course is usually 5K right?

00:55:18.640 --> 00:55:21.610
- It's a 5K, it's the one that
went around the tobacco field

00:55:21.610 --> 00:55:23.624
and down the dirt road
next to school and stuff

00:55:23.624 --> 00:55:26.440
and so they were like, okay well, day one,

00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:28.007
you guys are all just gonna run this 5K

00:55:28.007 --> 00:55:30.160
and we're gonna see where you're at.

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.890
- Yeah, baseline.

00:55:31.890 --> 00:55:34.010
- And because that was a competition

00:55:34.010 --> 00:55:35.450
amongst just the people,

00:55:35.450 --> 00:55:39.133
someone who you're now
half related to, Brian.

00:55:40.069 --> 00:55:42.759
Lewis's nephew I guess.

00:55:42.759 --> 00:55:44.307
- Oh yeah, yeah, okay, yeah,

00:55:44.307 --> 00:55:45.140
I know who you're talking about.

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:49.060
- Was out there, he's
another tall guy like me.

00:55:49.060 --> 00:55:52.540
And so we start running

00:55:52.540 --> 00:55:55.370
and I'm like, didn't
expect him to be good.

00:55:55.370 --> 00:55:57.160
Now he's tall like me but I just,

00:55:57.160 --> 00:55:59.710
he hadn't played any other
sports that I knew of.

00:55:59.710 --> 00:56:00.670
- Right.

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:03.640
- And I'm like well
he's not gonna beat me.

00:56:03.640 --> 00:56:04.496
- Oh.

00:56:04.496 --> 00:56:07.780
- No preparation for this.

00:56:07.780 --> 00:56:10.170
- I don't remember this
story, by the way, so--

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:13.630
- So I just, well I
gotta keep up with him.

00:56:13.630 --> 00:56:16.400
I'm like good lord, this guy is flying.

00:56:16.400 --> 00:56:18.700
- He was Blandining you.

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:21.040
- And I'm like out of shape.

00:56:21.040 --> 00:56:23.250
Basketball season was
over and I guess I was

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:24.740
in pretty good shape for
basketball season, whatever,

00:56:24.740 --> 00:56:28.230
but you know, not this kind of shape.

00:56:28.230 --> 00:56:30.020
- He was your height.

00:56:30.020 --> 00:56:32.260
- Yeah he was, so I'm
just running like crazy

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:33.880
and of course,

00:56:33.880 --> 00:56:36.820
the way that the course...

00:56:37.670 --> 00:56:38.617
I kept up with him the whole time

00:56:38.617 --> 00:56:41.410
and the way that the course
ends is it goes around one lap

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:43.483
of the track, you come
in and go around one,

00:56:43.483 --> 00:56:46.100
and I was like, he's not beating me.

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:47.390
- Ha.

00:56:47.390 --> 00:56:48.530
This is day one.

00:56:48.530 --> 00:56:51.520
- Day one, I ran.
- Did you talk to him?

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:53.390
- No, I ran--
- You were just running

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:55.237
beside him.
- I ran beside him and then--

00:56:55.237 --> 00:56:57.437
- And didn't say a word?
- In the last like

00:56:58.557 --> 00:57:00.706
10 steps, overtook him and beat him.

00:57:00.706 --> 00:57:03.630
- Were you sprinting at the end?

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:06.251
- I ran the 5K in 18 minutes.

00:57:06.251 --> 00:57:08.420
(Link chuckles)

00:57:08.420 --> 00:57:10.920
- That's pretty good.
- 18 minutes.

00:57:10.920 --> 00:57:12.350
- Just to beat Brian.

00:57:12.350 --> 00:57:14.620
- And which incidentally
is not even that good

00:57:14.620 --> 00:57:17.657
of a time in cross-country
once you start competing,

00:57:17.657 --> 00:57:20.931
but it was by far the best time I ever ran

00:57:20.931 --> 00:57:24.080
because I was like, I beat Brian

00:57:24.080 --> 00:57:25.821
and that's really all I wanted to prove

00:57:25.821 --> 00:57:28.500
and then the rest of the
time, during the meets,

00:57:28.500 --> 00:57:30.260
we would actually run
and then we would get

00:57:30.260 --> 00:57:34.290
around the corner and then we
would stop running and walk.

00:57:34.290 --> 00:57:35.980
- Break out potato chips or something?

00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:39.240
- And then I would, we would get back into

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:41.303
where the parents were watching.

00:57:42.250 --> 00:57:44.510
I had kinda just jogged along not too hard

00:57:44.510 --> 00:57:47.018
but I got to that place
where everybody was watching

00:57:47.018 --> 00:57:50.770
and I started sprinting and
I would pass like 17 people

00:57:50.770 --> 00:57:52.580
at the very end and
come in and finish like

00:57:52.580 --> 00:57:53.789
in the middle of the pack.

00:57:53.789 --> 00:57:56.819
Just so I could come in like
chariots of fire, just flying,

00:57:56.819 --> 00:57:59.720
like how does this tall
guy have so much energy?

00:57:59.720 --> 00:58:01.650
- You say chariots of
fire but I always picture

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:03.423
that guy in slow motion.

00:58:03.423 --> 00:58:07.247
- Yeah but I do look very slow
because of my limb length.

00:58:07.247 --> 00:58:08.610
(Link laughs)

00:58:08.610 --> 00:58:11.469
But beating Brian that first day--

00:58:11.469 --> 00:58:12.980
- In practice.
- In practice,

00:58:12.980 --> 00:58:15.096
that may be one of my
greatest physical feats.

00:58:15.096 --> 00:58:18.950
- You just wanted to
let him know you could.

00:58:18.950 --> 00:58:19.783
- Yeah 'cause I was thinking about this--

00:58:19.783 --> 00:58:22.785
- Because I once did.
- I was thinking about this--

00:58:22.785 --> 00:58:24.680
- That's your greatest physical feat,

00:58:24.680 --> 00:58:27.060
like that's the one you're thinking about.

00:58:27.060 --> 00:58:29.900
- Well I've had a lot of success in sports

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:32.892
but I don't think of those
as like a physical feat.

00:58:32.892 --> 00:58:35.130
- Yeah, it's not.
- You know what I'm saying.

00:58:35.130 --> 00:58:36.790
Just like a pure physical exertion.

00:58:36.790 --> 00:58:38.130
- Like pushing your body to the limit.

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.730
Have you ever been,
have you ever worked out

00:58:40.730 --> 00:58:42.169
at a gym to the point
where you just vomited

00:58:42.169 --> 00:58:43.340
on your own feet?
- No.

00:58:43.340 --> 00:58:46.360
I was thinking about
this and first of all,

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:48.970
it takes a lot for me to actually vomit.

00:58:48.970 --> 00:58:51.218
There's the McLaughlin
streak that me and my brother

00:58:51.218 --> 00:58:54.590
and my dad had for like, it
was like 40 years combined,

00:58:54.590 --> 00:58:56.580
nobody had vomited and
then within one year,

00:58:56.580 --> 00:58:58.463
we all three vomited.
- Oh.

00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:00.273
- But anyway.

00:59:00.273 --> 00:59:02.123
- I bet that was quite a gusher.

00:59:04.020 --> 00:59:08.100
- But I never worked out
so hard that I threw up.

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:09.510
I worked out hard but--

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:10.957
- Oh I came, when I first started going

00:59:10.957 --> 00:59:13.710
to the gym, I came close.

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:16.030
- 'Cause I used to go to
the same gym that you do--

00:59:16.030 --> 00:59:20.560
- They gave me a packet
like sugar water to drink.

00:59:20.560 --> 00:59:22.360
Had my head between my knees.

00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:26.070
- Well and it's like the
circuit training kinda thing

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:28.504
and we wore the heart
rate monitors and stuff

00:59:28.504 --> 00:59:31.504
and trying to burn a certain
number of calories or whatever.

00:59:33.199 --> 00:59:35.363
I mean basically it's like,

00:59:36.760 --> 00:59:37.793
a couple weeks ago,

00:59:39.060 --> 00:59:41.870
I go to a gym but I have a trainer

00:59:41.870 --> 00:59:45.580
and we go through a bunch
of different exercises.

00:59:45.580 --> 00:59:49.540
It's basically a mix of...

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:53.330
It's all strength
training but it's so quick

00:59:53.330 --> 00:59:54.940
and there's a bunch of
it going on together

00:59:54.940 --> 00:59:56.470
so you're also getting a cardio workout

00:59:56.470 --> 00:59:58.380
at the same time and it
lasts for like an hour,

00:59:58.380 --> 01:00:02.082
and I work out, like to the point,

01:00:02.082 --> 01:00:06.350
I have so much pride but
the bad kind of pride.

01:00:06.350 --> 01:00:10.650
Like I never ever stop

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:13.260
and say I can't do that last five seconds

01:00:13.260 --> 01:00:14.680
or something like that.

01:00:14.680 --> 01:00:16.120
There's just something in me.

01:00:16.120 --> 01:00:18.140
It's the whole Enneagram three thing.

01:00:18.140 --> 01:00:20.030
I cannot--

01:00:20.030 --> 01:00:21.885
- You can't admit.
- Right, I will not--

01:00:21.885 --> 01:00:23.020
- [Link] That you've reached the limit.

01:00:23.020 --> 01:00:24.260
If they've told you to do something.

01:00:24.260 --> 01:00:26.230
- You have to do this
plank with this weight

01:00:26.230 --> 01:00:28.790
on your back for a minute
after you've done this thing,

01:00:28.790 --> 01:00:31.510
I'm like, okay if that's
what it is, I'll do it.

01:00:31.510 --> 01:00:35.418
So she just pushes me as far as I'll go,

01:00:35.418 --> 01:00:37.696
and then I was like sore for like,

01:00:37.696 --> 01:00:40.040
that was probably as much
as I've pushed myself

01:00:40.040 --> 01:00:41.890
in my 40s definitely.

01:00:41.890 --> 01:00:43.670
Which my 40s haven't been very long,

01:00:43.670 --> 01:00:45.721
but then there's things like,

01:00:45.721 --> 01:00:48.660
I'll have to run across a parking lot

01:00:48.660 --> 01:00:52.550
in the rain to get to my car.

01:00:52.550 --> 01:00:56.290
It's just like, I got my back issues.

01:00:56.290 --> 01:00:58.440
There's four different places
that my back can get hurt

01:00:58.440 --> 01:01:00.490
and I've got my knee that's got arthritis

01:01:00.490 --> 01:01:03.296
and my elbow and my,
I've got tall guy issues,

01:01:03.296 --> 01:01:07.204
so I'm very stiff

01:01:07.204 --> 01:01:10.520
and very much thinking,
don't hurt yourself.

01:01:10.520 --> 01:01:12.340
Whenever I get going I'm
like don't hurt yourself.

01:01:12.340 --> 01:01:15.560
That's the main reason I'd
be scared of the Spartan Race

01:01:15.560 --> 01:01:18.880
'cause I was doing like assisted pull-ups

01:01:18.880 --> 01:01:22.423
at the gym and my upper back thing snaps.

01:01:23.314 --> 01:01:25.926
The rope climb would be a problem.

01:01:25.926 --> 01:01:28.360
I think I could get to a place

01:01:28.360 --> 01:01:29.790
where I was physically capable of it

01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:30.650
if I figured out how to do it

01:01:30.650 --> 01:01:33.860
but I think I could
potentially hurt myself but.

01:01:33.860 --> 01:01:36.070
- I mean you're talking about
running in the parking lot.

01:01:36.070 --> 01:01:39.150
I still have trouble, especially now,

01:01:39.150 --> 01:01:41.600
running in the parking
lot because of this medal.

01:01:42.610 --> 01:01:44.847
Wearing this everywhere
makes it a lot more difficult

01:01:44.847 --> 01:01:46.620
to exert myself.
- Do we need

01:01:46.620 --> 01:01:48.140
to install obstacles,

01:01:48.140 --> 01:01:52.953
like in the office, in order
for you to get to the bathroom,

01:01:52.953 --> 01:01:55.300
I'll get Blandine to come in here and--

01:01:55.300 --> 01:01:57.283
- Yeah we can jump over stuff.
- Yeah.

01:01:58.310 --> 01:02:00.908
- Sure, yeah.
- I'm very much

01:02:00.908 --> 01:02:04.980
attracted to the idea of doing those kinds

01:02:04.980 --> 01:02:06.100
of physical challenges.

01:02:06.100 --> 01:02:07.983
Not Blandine, I haven't seen Blandine.

01:02:10.460 --> 01:02:14.334
Because, well we started
working out basically

01:02:14.334 --> 01:02:16.190
as adults at the same time.

01:02:16.190 --> 01:02:19.630
I used to, I would play
basketball or do something

01:02:19.630 --> 01:02:21.150
and then you kinda just run out of time

01:02:21.150 --> 01:02:23.007
for doing that and then
there was like the years

01:02:23.007 --> 01:02:25.820
of inactivity where we were
just working really hard

01:02:25.820 --> 01:02:28.520
and I did convince us to
get that elliptical machine

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:29.543
that we had in the Lillington basement--

01:02:29.543 --> 01:02:30.510
- Yes.
- And we used it

01:02:30.510 --> 01:02:32.940
every once in awhile but--
- I never used that once.

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:35.203
- Then we just ended
up putting clothes on.

01:02:35.203 --> 01:02:38.024
- We would drink SlimFast.

01:02:38.024 --> 01:02:40.030
- But we drank SlimFast--
- But not to lose weight.

01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:41.100
- Just because it was efficient--

01:02:41.100 --> 01:02:44.550
- Just an efficient mode
of continuing to work

01:02:44.550 --> 01:02:48.090
but not have any sustenance.

01:02:48.090 --> 01:02:50.850
- In those days we would--
- We would down two SlimFasts

01:02:50.850 --> 01:02:52.940
and just keep editing
videos and sometimes you,

01:02:52.940 --> 01:02:54.830
one day you showed up with
an elliptical machine,

01:02:54.830 --> 01:02:56.770
I was like dude.

01:02:56.770 --> 01:02:59.940
Don't, that's just a--
- And I did use it.

01:02:59.940 --> 01:03:02.240
- A physical manifestation of my guilt.

01:03:02.240 --> 01:03:03.670
- I did use it.

01:03:03.670 --> 01:03:05.440
- No you didn't.
- Yeah I did.

01:03:05.440 --> 01:03:07.120
More than you did.
- Well yeah,

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:08.400
I never used it.
- Yeah exactly.

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:10.850
- You could use it once and
that statement would be true.

01:03:10.850 --> 01:03:13.010
- I used it at least 12 times.

01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:14.660
But we would have a SlimFast for lunch

01:03:14.660 --> 01:03:16.380
and then sometimes we'd
have a SlimFast for dinner

01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:18.388
as well and then we would keep
editing and then we would--

01:03:18.388 --> 01:03:19.221
- I think that is the
commercial by the way.

01:03:19.221 --> 01:03:22.636
- And then we would sleep at the office

01:03:22.636 --> 01:03:24.640
and then we'd wake up and do it again.

01:03:24.640 --> 01:03:27.910
When a project was due because
we didn't have any help.

01:03:27.910 --> 01:03:31.330
- And this is fresh in my mind because,

01:03:31.330 --> 01:03:33.730
I'll turn this into a
Mythical Society plug just

01:03:33.730 --> 01:03:36.013
because it's near and dear to my heart.

01:03:36.926 --> 01:03:41.926
I was watching, we're releasing
on the Mythical Society,

01:03:42.170 --> 01:03:46.190
we're trickling out the
archive of the original

01:03:46.190 --> 01:03:49.380
RhettandLinKast show which was a live show

01:03:49.380 --> 01:03:51.930
every Thursday night on Ustream.

01:03:51.930 --> 01:03:53.490
Now it's just something you can watch

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.290
if you're a Mythical Society member.

01:03:56.562 --> 01:04:00.020
And so we released the
first few of those and

01:04:01.535 --> 01:04:04.400
there's one that features that elliptical.

01:04:04.400 --> 01:04:08.230
'Cause I remember you got
it and we did a whole show

01:04:08.230 --> 01:04:11.951
about exercise or something
and I'm pretty sure

01:04:11.951 --> 01:04:13.644
that you were on the elliptical.

01:04:13.644 --> 01:04:16.030
That one hasn't been
released to the Society yet

01:04:16.030 --> 01:04:17.870
but just looking at that basement

01:04:17.870 --> 01:04:19.610
and seeing how we were
setting up everything,

01:04:19.610 --> 01:04:21.230
it just reminded me of that.

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:25.582
Basically just a shameless
Mythical Society plug but--

01:04:25.582 --> 01:04:28.783
- No but I definitely have a,

01:04:29.804 --> 01:04:32.120
as you start getting
older and you're like,

01:04:32.120 --> 01:04:33.273
I gotta do something to counteract

01:04:33.273 --> 01:04:35.260
what is happening to my body.

01:04:35.260 --> 01:04:38.110
I mean the main thing that's
happening to my body is just

01:04:40.050 --> 01:04:41.880
annoying injuries

01:04:43.645 --> 01:04:45.420
and you don't even know
what happened, you're like,

01:04:45.420 --> 01:04:47.210
I don't remember doing
anything to my shoulder,

01:04:47.210 --> 01:04:49.890
I think it's just, it's expired.

01:04:49.890 --> 01:04:54.220
- Well my, I was playing
soccer over Thanksgiving

01:04:54.220 --> 01:04:58.080
with my nephew Nehemiah and the kids

01:04:58.080 --> 01:05:01.373
and I kicked a soccer ball
and I messed up my hip flexor.

01:05:01.373 --> 01:05:02.813
- Oh yeah you can't do anything like that.

01:05:02.813 --> 01:05:05.237
- I just, I walloped that soccer ball

01:05:05.237 --> 01:05:07.640
and I knew something had happened.

01:05:07.640 --> 01:05:09.850
And it's still not better.
- You gotta stretch.

01:05:09.850 --> 01:05:11.780
- I've been stretching.
- You gotta stretch one minute

01:05:11.780 --> 01:05:13.800
for every year that you're old.

01:05:13.800 --> 01:05:16.060
So you gotta do, I gotta
do a 41 minute stretch.

01:05:16.060 --> 01:05:17.560
- Every morning?

01:05:17.560 --> 01:05:19.070
- No before any physical activity.

01:05:19.070 --> 01:05:21.010
- Yeah.
- But I do like

01:05:21.010 --> 01:05:22.293
a 20 minute stretch,

01:05:24.590 --> 01:05:25.980
as my wife calls it now, the stretch

01:05:25.980 --> 01:05:27.620
and snuggle time with Barbara.

01:05:27.620 --> 01:05:29.950
- Here's my next thing because

01:05:31.890 --> 01:05:35.113
the Spartan thing, I do
recommend it, I think it's,

01:05:36.100 --> 01:05:38.370
I don't know, there is
a lot of risk for injury

01:05:38.370 --> 01:05:40.910
so you gotta be, we need to be careful.

01:05:40.910 --> 01:05:43.170
- There's less risk in triathlons though.

01:05:43.170 --> 01:05:46.110
- Well somebody--
- That's just endurance.

01:05:46.110 --> 01:05:47.523
- Is that where you swim?
- Swim, bike and run.

01:05:47.523 --> 01:05:49.661
- Like an Iron Man?
- But you could do

01:05:49.661 --> 01:05:51.390
short versions of those.

01:05:51.390 --> 01:05:53.130
- The swimming is the problem for me.

01:05:53.130 --> 01:05:56.530
I woke up in the middle
of the night thirsty

01:05:56.530 --> 01:05:58.320
as all get out last night.

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:01.070
And you know I keep a big thing of water

01:06:01.070 --> 01:06:02.210
beside my bed--
- If you swim,

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:03.621
you don't get thirsty--
- I'm a Neal.

01:06:03.621 --> 01:06:04.560
- 'Cause you just absorb all the water.

01:06:04.560 --> 01:06:06.406
- And I gotta stay hydrated.

01:06:06.406 --> 01:06:09.728
No, I start drinking the
water and I'm so thirsty

01:06:09.728 --> 01:06:13.290
that I'm like, I didn't take a breath in

01:06:13.290 --> 01:06:14.730
before I started drinking the water,

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:17.154
I had exhaled and then I
started drinking the water.

01:06:17.154 --> 01:06:19.975
(slurping)

01:06:19.975 --> 01:06:23.030
And I was so thirsty that I
was having this inner dialogue

01:06:23.030 --> 01:06:24.500
of I need to breathe now,

01:06:24.500 --> 01:06:27.916
but I'm gonna get one more
slurp in of this water

01:06:27.916 --> 01:06:32.916
and I did that and then I
nearly had a panic attack

01:06:33.410 --> 01:06:37.370
that I was gonna drown myself.

01:06:37.370 --> 01:06:42.183
I just have this fear of drowning.

01:06:43.140 --> 01:06:44.438
- Drowning in your own Thermos.

01:06:44.438 --> 01:06:45.950
(Link chuckles)

01:06:45.950 --> 01:06:48.011
- I was afraid.

01:06:48.011 --> 01:06:50.110
I don't like holding my breath, man,

01:06:50.110 --> 01:06:53.560
and that's why I cannot,
if I'm gonna drown

01:06:53.560 --> 01:06:56.260
in a water bottle at 3 a.m.,
how am I supposed to get

01:06:56.260 --> 01:06:59.170
in the ocean and swim
for a prolonged period?

01:06:59.170 --> 01:07:00.710
I cannot do that.

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:02.740
So that's the next thing I'm gonna do.

01:07:02.740 --> 01:07:05.190
- Triathlon?
- I'm gonna be a Iron Man.

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:07.140
- If you wanna do
triathlons, I'm totally down

01:07:07.140 --> 01:07:09.360
'cause I think it's much less
likely that I get injured

01:07:09.360 --> 01:07:11.730
'cause I'm not pulling
on things and crawling,

01:07:11.730 --> 01:07:14.127
it's just running, biking, swimming.

01:07:14.127 --> 01:07:15.570
And I don't know what the order is.

01:07:15.570 --> 01:07:18.250
I think it's biking, swimming, running?

01:07:18.250 --> 01:07:19.990
- Biking, swimming, running.

01:07:19.990 --> 01:07:23.112
Oh, I'm not ready to do swim training.

01:07:23.112 --> 01:07:24.920
But--
- Hey.

01:07:24.920 --> 01:07:26.240
Swimming.

01:07:26.240 --> 01:07:27.500
- That's something you
do when you're like 80.

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:29.946
- Is--
- You can do that.

01:07:29.946 --> 01:07:32.100
- No, swimming is tough.

01:07:32.100 --> 01:07:34.010
Okay, so we used to live next to--

01:07:34.010 --> 01:07:35.460
- I'm talking about water aerobics.

01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:38.380
- The pool--
- I've never done that.

01:07:38.380 --> 01:07:40.170
- In Sherman Oaks and I
was like, I'm gonna get up

01:07:40.170 --> 01:07:43.210
in the morning and just swim some laps.

01:07:43.210 --> 01:07:46.210
I was a good swimmer, I was a
competitive swimmer growing up

01:07:47.805 --> 01:07:52.020
and was good, should have stuck with it.

01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:53.660
But--
- So that now,

01:07:53.660 --> 01:07:55.031
you would be what?

01:07:55.031 --> 01:07:56.980
A swimmer?
- So that I would be

01:07:56.980 --> 01:07:58.542
Michael Phelps, no.

01:07:58.542 --> 01:07:59.847
'Cause I was actually good at it.

01:07:59.847 --> 01:08:01.850
I think I was actually
naturally better at it

01:08:01.850 --> 01:08:05.163
than other things that I did
but I didn't like the Speedo.

01:08:06.720 --> 01:08:09.010
But I love Speedos now,
that's the weird thing.

01:08:09.010 --> 01:08:10.310
I'm wearing one right now.

01:08:11.710 --> 01:08:13.580
But I was like--
- Not a sponsor.

01:08:13.580 --> 01:08:15.447
- I'm gonna go and--
- It's a brand.

01:08:15.447 --> 01:08:17.080
- And just swim some laps

01:08:18.210 --> 01:08:21.470
and I started out in freestyle,

01:08:21.470 --> 01:08:23.575
thinking that, okay, this is the easiest,

01:08:23.575 --> 01:08:24.993
this is pretty easy.

01:08:26.060 --> 01:08:29.850
A couple of back and
forths and you're like,

01:08:29.850 --> 01:08:33.360
whoa, this is not easy, man.

01:08:33.360 --> 01:08:34.623
Talk about gettin' your breath right.

01:08:34.623 --> 01:08:37.520
You end up having to take
a breath like every time,

01:08:37.520 --> 01:08:42.299
'cause I was just a
sprint swimmer so I would

01:08:42.299 --> 01:08:45.840
go under, I would dive in and
I wouldn't come up at all.

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:49.640
You know what I'm saying,
I would go, do a turn,

01:08:49.640 --> 01:08:51.753
maybe take one breath down and back

01:08:51.753 --> 01:08:54.760
in a 50 meter or whatever,
right, but like--

01:08:54.760 --> 01:08:56.790
- You do a freestyle but
your face would stay under.

01:08:56.790 --> 01:08:59.130
- Yeah, but this was like,

01:08:59.130 --> 01:09:01.977
I gotta come up every freaking
time, I'm so out of shape.

01:09:01.977 --> 01:09:03.250
I'm in a little bit better shape now

01:09:03.250 --> 01:09:05.100
but then I started going
to the breast stroke

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:06.140
and I was--
- Okay breast stroke.

01:09:06.140 --> 01:09:07.590
- Like ooh, gotta do breast stroke,

01:09:07.590 --> 01:09:09.340
I gotta keep my head
above water the whole time

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:10.400
or I'm gonna die.

01:09:10.400 --> 01:09:12.150
- Then after that you were
doing like back stroke.

01:09:12.150 --> 01:09:13.810
- Then after that I got out
of the pool and went home

01:09:13.810 --> 01:09:15.093
and never did it again.

01:09:15.093 --> 01:09:16.280
(Link chuckles)

01:09:16.280 --> 01:09:19.856
But it's tough, it's really tough.

01:09:19.856 --> 01:09:22.580
- If it was muddy water,
it would probably be fun.

01:09:22.580 --> 01:09:24.860
- But with everything like that, you,

01:09:24.860 --> 01:09:28.083
like with your running, you
get to a place where it clicks

01:09:28.083 --> 01:09:30.778
and you're like, okay, I'm normalized.

01:09:30.778 --> 01:09:33.953
'Cause with every physical
activity, you hit that wall,

01:09:33.953 --> 01:09:38.183
you get through that wall,
then if you get your breathing

01:09:38.183 --> 01:09:41.060
and everything in rhythm and
you're not worrying so much

01:09:41.060 --> 01:09:42.200
about competing against somebody else

01:09:42.200 --> 01:09:45.110
but competing against yourself,
you can kinda maintain it

01:09:45.110 --> 01:09:46.443
for a lot longer than you realize.

01:09:46.443 --> 01:09:49.370
I'd love to do that man,
let's be triathlon buddies

01:09:49.370 --> 01:09:52.720
and call ourselves the triathlon guys.

01:09:52.720 --> 01:09:55.060
Shorten it to tri guys, no, taken.

01:09:55.060 --> 01:09:57.887
- Well I already have a workout partner.

01:09:57.887 --> 01:10:00.080
- Blandine, well can we be a trio?

01:10:00.080 --> 01:10:03.660
- No but--
- The triple tri people.

01:10:03.660 --> 01:10:05.310
- You can probably look up Brian.

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:08.230
He's probably still your height.

01:10:08.230 --> 01:10:10.403
- Yeah.
- I think he works

01:10:10.403 --> 01:10:13.183
in an auction, he works for--

01:10:13.183 --> 01:10:14.883
- He's an auctioneer?
- Christie's.

01:10:14.883 --> 01:10:17.970
Isn't that the fancy auction place?

01:10:17.970 --> 01:10:19.060
- He's like in New York City right now.

01:10:19.060 --> 01:10:20.190
- Yeah, yeah.

01:10:20.190 --> 01:10:22.120
- New York City.

01:10:22.120 --> 01:10:24.150
- New York City.

01:10:24.150 --> 01:10:26.090
Brian's in New York City?

01:10:26.090 --> 01:10:27.462
- Working for Christie.

01:10:27.462 --> 01:10:29.720
- Working for Christie's.

01:10:29.720 --> 01:10:32.120
Christie's Auction House.

01:10:32.120 --> 01:10:33.613
Candelabras and such.

01:10:35.160 --> 01:10:36.440
Okay.
- That might be difficult

01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:37.273
being on opposite sides of the country.

01:10:37.273 --> 01:10:39.568
- So there it is, my
greatest physical feat

01:10:39.568 --> 01:10:41.490
and I have this to show for it,

01:10:41.490 --> 01:10:43.363
the thing that slammed me in the face.

01:10:43.363 --> 01:10:44.550
(chuckles)

01:10:44.550 --> 01:10:46.370
I'm a Spartan.

01:10:46.370 --> 01:10:48.070
I'm a Spartan.
- I'm envious, man.

01:10:48.070 --> 01:10:50.540
- Kiko, I'm gonna give
you all types of pictures.

01:10:50.540 --> 01:10:53.020
They were taking pictures during the race

01:10:53.020 --> 01:10:55.650
and so I tried to make a point

01:10:57.226 --> 01:10:58.810
to make faces.

01:10:58.810 --> 01:11:02.523
- Maybe I could be a Spartan photographer.

01:11:02.523 --> 01:11:04.280
- Phortographer.
(Rhett chuckles)

01:11:04.280 --> 01:11:06.610
- Yeah, that could be my position.

01:11:06.610 --> 01:11:09.730
I can get quite an angle
on things with my height.

01:11:09.730 --> 01:11:12.370
- Thanks for sharing in
this physical milestone

01:11:12.370 --> 01:11:13.723
experience of mine.

01:11:14.930 --> 01:11:18.970
I'll show you how to climb a
rope if you ever wanna learn.

01:11:18.970 --> 01:11:20.103
I did it once.

01:11:21.317 --> 01:11:22.917
And there you have it.

01:11:22.917 --> 01:11:26.088
#EarBiscuits, if you got
any questions about like

01:11:26.088 --> 01:11:29.560
pushing yourself to the
physical limit, I'm here.

01:11:29.560 --> 01:11:30.487
I'm here for you.

01:11:31.760 --> 01:11:34.160
Ask away.
- In the meantime--

01:11:34.160 --> 01:11:35.970
- Oh we need a recommendation.

01:11:35.970 --> 01:11:36.803
- Oh yeah I'm gonna give

01:11:36.803 --> 01:11:38.120
a recommendation.
- Don't forget that.

01:11:38.120 --> 01:11:39.890
- It's a little bit thematic

01:11:41.741 --> 01:11:43.460
with what we've been talking about.

01:11:43.460 --> 01:11:44.593
- Recs and effects.

01:11:45.832 --> 01:11:48.160
Share it, baby.
- I'm gonna recommend a book

01:11:48.160 --> 01:11:52.200
that I finished in two days.

01:11:52.200 --> 01:11:54.803
So it's an easy read.
- Is it ours?

01:11:56.180 --> 01:11:58.970
- No, I think, I do recommend our book.

01:11:58.970 --> 01:12:01.620
I definitely recommend The
Lost Causes of Bleak Creek

01:12:02.758 --> 01:12:04.706
but this was actually--
- Is it out yet?

01:12:04.706 --> 01:12:05.960
Have you had to--

01:12:05.960 --> 01:12:07.414
- Is it out yet?
- Yeah.

01:12:07.414 --> 01:12:12.234
- BleakCreek.com is the website
where you can pick that up.

01:12:12.234 --> 01:12:16.040
You can pre-order that.
- Nice plug, Rhett.

01:12:16.040 --> 01:12:17.610
You just recommended our own book.

01:12:17.610 --> 01:12:18.570
- That wasn't my intention.

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:20.920
My intention was to recommend
a book that is kind of,

01:12:20.920 --> 01:12:22.540
it's a little bit of an older book,

01:12:22.540 --> 01:12:23.800
like 20 years or whatever but--

01:12:23.800 --> 01:12:24.633
- Animal Farm?

01:12:25.990 --> 01:12:27.625
- I read The Road--

01:12:27.625 --> 01:12:29.944
- The Road.
- By Cormac McCarthy.

01:12:29.944 --> 01:12:32.910
So I didn't--
- Viggo was in the movie

01:12:32.910 --> 01:12:34.110
version of that, right?

01:12:34.110 --> 01:12:38.480
- Yeah he was, I didn't realize how much,

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:41.806
Cormac McCarthy, one of
the great American authors

01:12:41.806 --> 01:12:44.420
has written a bunch of different stuff.

01:12:44.420 --> 01:12:46.790
All the Pretty Horses,
No Country for Old Men,

01:12:46.790 --> 01:12:49.063
things that have been
made into epic movies.

01:12:51.038 --> 01:12:56.038
But The Road is like, I
mean you can, it's short.

01:12:56.358 --> 01:12:59.533
You can read it in a sitting
if you've got enough time.

01:13:00.763 --> 01:13:05.763
But it is so just, speaking
of bleak. (chuckles)

01:13:06.840 --> 01:13:11.560
It is a just bleak book.

01:13:11.560 --> 01:13:16.560
Near future, post-apocalyptic, just gray,

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:20.500
dull landscape, no people and the people

01:13:20.500 --> 01:13:23.670
that are around are just garbage.

01:13:23.670 --> 01:13:25.590
- You're making it sound great.

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:29.893
- It's exactly the kinda thing
that turns me on. (laughs)

01:13:31.360 --> 01:13:32.193
- Okay.

01:13:32.193 --> 01:13:36.076
- So if you're like, if
you like a book about--

01:13:36.076 --> 01:13:38.750
- If you're aroused by the apocalypse.

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:41.350
- If you're aroused by a
world where there's no hope

01:13:42.240 --> 01:13:46.287
like I am, if that just
scratches the right itch for you,

01:13:46.287 --> 01:13:48.400
and listen, I know you're out there.

01:13:48.400 --> 01:13:49.590
I know a lot of you are out there,

01:13:49.590 --> 01:13:51.490
that's why these books are so popular.

01:13:52.339 --> 01:13:54.690
Then I highly recommend that.

01:13:54.690 --> 01:13:57.863
And I'm gettin' into, now I'm
reading All the Pretty Horses.

01:13:58.837 --> 01:14:01.170
I'm gonna go through the whole collection

01:14:02.325 --> 01:14:03.820
because--
- Hold on.

01:14:03.820 --> 01:14:06.480
You made your recommendation--
- Listen.

01:14:06.480 --> 01:14:08.300
- It takes the punch out
of the recommendation

01:14:08.300 --> 01:14:12.160
to just slather it out--
- Here's an interesting fact

01:14:12.160 --> 01:14:14.900
about Cormac McCarthy and I
didn't realize that this was,

01:14:14.900 --> 01:14:16.989
I thought that maybe it
was just a stylistic choice

01:14:16.989 --> 01:14:21.989
for this particular book but
he does not use punctuation.

01:14:22.440 --> 01:14:26.100
Nearly to the degree of other
people so in other words,

01:14:26.100 --> 01:14:27.043
there's no punctuation.

01:14:27.043 --> 01:14:29.750
There's no quotation
marks in the entire book.

01:14:29.750 --> 01:14:30.970
- Are there periods?

01:14:30.970 --> 01:14:32.940
- There are periods but there are--

01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:35.560
- Commas?
- Very few commas.

01:14:35.560 --> 01:14:38.500
So one of the things that he
did back in the day, he's old,

01:14:38.500 --> 01:14:40.870
he's like almost in his 90s at this point

01:14:40.870 --> 01:14:45.870
but his story goes that
he was tasked with sort of

01:14:47.120 --> 01:14:50.424
cleaning up some writers' work
back when he was a young man

01:14:50.424 --> 01:14:52.670
and one of the things he
did is he went through

01:14:52.670 --> 01:14:55.260
and he took out a bunch of what he called

01:14:55.260 --> 01:14:57.794
unnecessary punctuation
including a bunch of commas

01:14:57.794 --> 01:15:00.660
and the guy was like I really like this,

01:15:00.660 --> 01:15:04.733
how efficient this is, and
now his writing style is,

01:15:06.752 --> 01:15:08.133
he'll just be like,

01:15:09.780 --> 01:15:12.940
why don't we go do a Spartan Race period.

01:15:12.940 --> 01:15:15.506
Link said, period.
- Oh.

01:15:15.506 --> 01:15:17.970
'Cause Link said is just, is a sentence.

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:19.251
- Yeah.
- It can be.

01:15:19.251 --> 01:15:21.560
- But usually.
- That is odd.

01:15:21.560 --> 01:15:25.020
- It is, at first a little bit like, oh,

01:15:25.020 --> 01:15:27.735
am I reading someone's thoughts?

01:15:27.735 --> 01:15:29.847
And then you're just
like no, you know what.

01:15:29.847 --> 01:15:32.910
Punctuation is unnecessary and then

01:15:32.910 --> 01:15:37.570
there's these whole long exchanges

01:15:37.570 --> 01:15:40.380
which is like in The Road
it's a father and son talking

01:15:40.380 --> 01:15:42.530
to each other and he
doesn't say the son said

01:15:42.530 --> 01:15:45.263
or, and first of all, it's
just the boy and the man.

01:15:46.410 --> 01:15:47.710
He doesn't give 'em names.

01:15:49.054 --> 01:15:52.068
He just completely forgets
saying this person--

01:15:52.068 --> 01:15:52.901
- You don't know who's saying what?

01:15:52.901 --> 01:15:53.975
- No, you do know.

01:15:53.975 --> 01:15:56.610
You do know who's saying
what because of the way

01:15:56.610 --> 01:15:57.847
that it's written.

01:15:57.847 --> 01:16:00.240
When it's a two person
exchange it's usually just back

01:16:00.240 --> 01:16:01.890
and forth but--
- But if you get it off,

01:16:01.890 --> 01:16:03.160
then you're backwards.

01:16:03.160 --> 01:16:05.150
- But it doesn't happen.

01:16:05.150 --> 01:16:06.890
I don't know, it was kind of a trip.

01:16:06.890 --> 01:16:08.470
I love discovering something

01:16:08.470 --> 01:16:10.590
that's like an American treasure

01:16:10.590 --> 01:16:12.815
that a lot of people already know about

01:16:12.815 --> 01:16:14.725
and then being like oh.

01:16:14.725 --> 01:16:16.593
It's like when you find
a new television show

01:16:16.593 --> 01:16:19.190
and you're like there's
seven seasons of this.

01:16:19.190 --> 01:16:20.023
- Oh.

01:16:20.023 --> 01:16:21.310
- So then I'm like oh.
- Good rec.

01:16:21.310 --> 01:16:23.100
- You know, so--
- Good rec.

01:16:23.100 --> 01:16:25.100
- I'm excited about that.

